  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-LBN102</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainability of enterprises strengthened with focus on entrepreneurship development, access to finance, market systems development and the social and solidarity economy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon&#x2019;s prolonged economic and institutional crisis, compounded by conflict and regional instability, continues to weaken enterprises, employment, livelihoods and productive sectors, particularly affecting SMEs, cooperatives, informal workers, women, youth, refugees and vulnerable host communities. During the biennium, the ILO will support sustainable enterprise development as both a livelihood response and a contribution to longer-term structural transformation towards a more productive, resilient and export-oriented economy capable of generating productive and decent work. Expected results include strengthened business development and financial services; improved access to tailored finance and markets; upgraded value chains; stronger cooperative information and management systems; and enhanced integration of social sustainability and business resilience into enterprise support. The programme will also strengthen the knowledge base required for future employment-focused economic and enterprise policies by supporting integrated approaches that link trade, enterprise, labour market and occupational data to inform policies on productive sectors, skills development and decent work creation. Beneficiaries include SMEs, cooperatives, workers and entrepreneurs in the informal economy, women, youth, refugees, vulnerable Lebanese host communities and actors in priority productive sectors. The strategy will be implemented with relevant government institutions, employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations, cooperatives, financial and business development service providers, UN agencies and development partners, without naming local implementing partners, in support of inclusive recovery, enterprise resilience and sustainable employment creation.</narrative>
  </description>
